{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for task/worktree-visibility at exact head ad5e0c6636b56320fd0a48194aadf75c48a773f7. Head and clean status verified; probes deleted and the worktree left clean. Gates are green: vet, test, -race, build, ui npm ci, ui build, ui test (12/12). Disclosure: a delegate ran the sweep under my direction; I re-derived every must-fix below myself before signing, and I mark below what I checked by my own execution.\n\nMUST FIX.\n\n(1) The visibility path mutates and locks the user's checkouts. app.go:135 and :191 call `git worktree list` and `git status --porcelain=v1` without --no-optional-locks, so every poll refreshes and rewrites .git/index under index.lock — while the doc comment at app.go:130 says \"without writing anything to git\". I verified this by execution: index mtime 946702800 -\u003e 1786152564 after that exact command, and unchanged when --no-optional-locks is prepended. The UI polls /v0/worktrees every 5s and LocalWorktrees fans out one status per worktree, so on this repository that is 19 index rewrites per 5 seconds, forever, and a concurrent commit/add/checkout/rebase in any of them can fail on index.lock. Add --no-optional-locks to both calls, and cache or debounce the fan-out.\n\n(2) Transitive provenance fabricates a commitment association and labels it an exact match. worktrees.ts:71 dependsOnAny walks the whole rests-on DAG with no stopping rule, so any artifact that transitively reaches an anchor contributes its commit to expectedHeads. The ordinary \"next, do X\" pattern — a later request resting on the previous report — is enough. The delegate reproduced it against the real module: an unrelated commitment's checkout appeared under mine with headMatches true. I confirmed the absence of any boundary in the walk by reading. Stop at any statement of kind request/promise/report that is not itself an anchor.\n\n(3) Current is wrong whenever gs serve is not started from the repository toplevel. canonicalPath(w.Repo) at app.go:181 resolves the raw user string, and gs serve defaults --repo \".\" (main.go:81) — I confirmed both by reading, and the delegate confirmed by execution that serving from a subdirectory marks no checkout as current and loses the current-sorts-first ordering. Resolve via rev-parse --show-toplevel. Note the branch's tests pass absolute toplevel paths, a state easier than reality, which is why they miss it.\n\n(4) Branch is overloaded with the literal string \"detached\" (app.go:165), which is also a legal branch name. Verified by reading. Three consequences the delegate reproduced: the UI renders it as a branch; a commitment declaring body.branch \"detached\" associates every detached checkout; and the React key branch:checkout collides for two detached worktrees sharing a basename. Carry a separate Detached bool and leave Branch empty.\n\n(5) Bare repositories are reported as \"unborn\", and prunable and locked are parsed away. The switch handles worktree/HEAD/branch/detached and nothing else — verified by reading. A bare main worktree emits `bare` with no HEAD and no branch, so it renders as \"unborn @no HEAD\", a plain misstatement of repository state; and a worktree whose directory is gone keeps a stale branch and HEAD behind the same \"unavailable\" badge a transient read error produces, even though git said `prunable gitdir file points to non-existent location`. Parse bare/locked/prunable into distinct states so permanently-gone is not confused with unreadable.\n\nSHOULD FIX. (6) body.branch/head/commit is a new wire convention with a consumer, no producer, and no documentation anywhere in spike/, ui/, docs/, SKILL.md or AGENTS.md; the two UI tests asserting on it are the only place that state exists. Document and emit it, or drop the hints. (7) Rests-On: trailer associations are unauthenticated free text — any local branch can name any event id and appear under that commitment, presented at the same confidence as signed durable facts. At minimum require the commit author to match the performer, or badge trailer-derived rows unverified. (8) Those associations vanish silently past Store.Graph's 80-commit limit. (9) The \"not durable\" caveat is hover-only on the inline pills, so a green \"clean @sha\" under a Review row reads as durable evidence — which compounds 2 and 7. (10) No cache, debounce, or per-worktree timeout on the fan-out. (11) headMatches goes silent when two heads are declared: expectedHead is set only when expectedHeads.size === 1 (worktrees.ts:62), so with a promise head and a report head the red \"moved\" badge never appears even though the checkout matches neither — verified by reading. (12) The path-privacy regression test checks only the serving repo's own path against a single-worktree fixture, so a regression emitting linked worktree paths would still pass it.\n\nWHAT HELD. Condition 2 — absolute-path privacy — survived the attacks I care most about, and this is the part of the branch I would keep unchanged: Checkout is filepath.Base, status output is only length-tested, and exec's ExitError renders as \"exit status N\" with stderr captured and never written, confirmed against both a non-repo and a missing directory. Nothing in the diff writes a durable act, and WorktreeView appears in neither Snapshot nor Projection, so condition 1's separation is structurally sound apart from the presentation issue in (9). The -z parser is correct on git 2.50.1 including paths with spaces. The generated UI is reproducible: two builds diffed byte-identical. Grouping is a total partition of the three open statuses with no silent loss. One residual worth recording rather than fixing here: /v0/worktrees has no per-actor gating, so every actor session on a multi-actor custodian sees the operator's basenames, branch names and unpublished head SHAs — consistent with the pre-existing /v0/graph, so not a regression of this branch."}